1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,971
A steep bicycle climb.

2
00:00:01,971 --> 00:00:04,478
Your chest heaves and you breathe hard.

3
00:00:04,478 --> 00:00:07,645
A tall tree beside the
road has no lungs at all.

4
00:00:07,645 --> 00:00:10,907
Soaked seeds in a jar even
warm up as they sprout.

5
00:00:11,307 --> 00:00:13,543
You breathe to get energy from food.

6
00:00:13,543 --> 00:00:15,272
Does the tree do the same?

7
00:00:15,722 --> 00:00:18,323
Aarav wondered this at
the top of the climb.

8
00:00:18,323 --> 00:00:20,106
He asked: do plants breathe?

9
00:00:20,106 --> 00:00:22,759
And how does a cell get
energy out of sugar?

10
00:00:23,109 --> 00:00:24,907
Here is the short answer.

11
00:00:24,907 --> 00:00:27,713
Yes, plants respire, through tiny pores.

12
00:00:27,713 --> 00:00:32,378
And every cell starts by splitting
glucose in a process called glycolysis.

13
00:00:32,878 --> 00:00:37,364
By the end, you will know what
respiration is, how plants exchange gases,

14
00:00:37,364 --> 00:00:41,719
the ten steps of glycolysis in five
moves, and how much ATP it makes.

15
00:00:42,369 --> 00:00:44,290
First, what is respiration?

16
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:48,407
Food holds energy in the bonds
between its carbon atoms.

17
00:00:48,407 --> 00:00:52,473
Breaking these bonds by oxidation
inside cells releases energy.

18
00:00:52,473 --> 00:00:54,475
This is cellular respiration.

19
00:00:54,925 --> 00:00:57,515
The energy is not used all at once.

20
00:00:57,515 --> 00:01:01,390
It is trapped in ATP, the
energy currency of the cell.

21
00:01:01,390 --> 00:01:05,296
Glucose and oxygen give carbon
dioxide, water and energy.

22
00:01:05,946 --> 00:01:07,650
So, do plants breathe?

23
00:01:07,650 --> 00:01:08,739
Let's zoom in.

24
00:01:09,089 --> 00:01:10,625
A plant has no lungs.

25
00:01:10,625 --> 00:01:15,514
Each part, the roots, stem and leaves,
takes care of its own gas exchange.

26
00:01:16,014 --> 00:01:17,353
Zoom into a leaf.

27
00:01:17,353 --> 00:01:21,247
Its cells are loosely packed,
with air spaces between them.

28
00:01:21,247 --> 00:01:23,704
Almost every living cell touches air.

29
00:01:24,204 --> 00:01:25,529
Zoom to the surface.

30
00:01:25,529 --> 00:01:29,493
Gases move through stomata on leaves,
and through lenticels on stems.

31
00:01:29,493 --> 00:01:32,675
So yes, plants respire, but
without breathing organs.

32
00:01:33,325 --> 00:01:34,583
Now glycolysis.

33
00:01:34,583 --> 00:01:38,392
Follow one glucose in the
cytoplasm, step by step.

34
00:01:38,742 --> 00:01:39,642
Step one.

35
00:01:39,455 --> 00:01:42,806
Glucose, a six carbon sugar,
takes a phosphate from ATP.

36
00:01:42,806 --> 00:01:44,320
The enzyme is hexokinase.

37
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:46,234
It becomes glucose six phosphate.

38
00:01:46,984 --> 00:01:47,884
Step two.

39
00:01:47,657 --> 00:01:49,973
It is rearranged to
fructose six phosphate.

40
00:01:49,973 --> 00:01:52,042
A second ATP adds one more phosphate.

41
00:01:52,042 --> 00:01:54,234
Now it is fructose one six bisphosphate.

42
00:01:54,984 --> 00:01:55,884
Step three.

43
00:01:55,699 --> 00:01:59,007
This sugar splits into two three
carbon molecules, PGAL and DHAP.

44
00:01:59,007 --> 00:02:01,686
DHAP changes into PGAL
too, so we follow two PGAL.

45
00:02:02,436 --> 00:02:03,336
Step four.

46
00:02:03,036 --> 00:02:05,802
Each PGAL gives two hydrogen
atoms to NAD plus, making NADH.

47
00:02:05,802 --> 00:02:06,702
It becomes BPGA.

48
00:02:06,651 --> 00:02:08,534
Then BPGA becomes PGA and makes one ATP.

49
00:02:09,284 --> 00:02:10,184
Step five.

50
00:02:09,981 --> 00:02:13,570
A few steps later, PEP becomes
pyruvic acid and makes one more ATP.

51
00:02:13,570 --> 00:02:15,461
One glucose gives two pyruvic acid.

52
00:02:16,211 --> 00:02:17,111
Remember this.

53
00:02:17,102 --> 00:02:19,068
One glucose gives two pyruvic acid.

54
00:02:19,668 --> 00:02:20,775
Now count the ATP.

55
00:02:21,125 --> 00:02:24,869
Two ATP are used at the start,
one at each phosphate step.

56
00:02:25,319 --> 00:02:28,169
Each PGAL makes two ATP,
and there are two PGAL.

57
00:02:28,169 --> 00:02:29,530
So four ATP are made.

58
00:02:29,530 --> 00:02:33,784
Four made, minus two used, leaves a
net gain of two ATP, plus two NADH.

59
00:02:34,434 --> 00:02:36,604
Who runs glycolysis, and where?

60
00:02:36,954 --> 00:02:40,691
It happens in the cytoplasm
of all living organisms.

61
00:02:40,691 --> 00:02:45,740
In organisms that live without
oxygen, it is the only way to respire.

62
00:02:46,190 --> 00:02:50,356
It is also called the EMP pathway,
after Gustav Embden, Otto Meyerhof and

63
00:02:50,356 --> 00:02:51,256
J.

64
00:02:50,721 --> 00:02:51,621
Parnas.

65
00:02:51,309 --> 00:02:53,223
Plants get glucose from sucrose.

66
00:02:53,223 --> 00:02:56,686
The enzyme invertase splits
sucrose into glucose and fructose.

67
00:02:57,336 --> 00:02:59,458
What happens to pyruvic acid next?

68
00:02:59,458 --> 00:03:00,780
It has three roads.

69
00:03:01,130 --> 00:03:06,052
Without oxygen, it can become lactic
acid, as in our muscles, or ethanol

70
00:03:06,052 --> 00:03:08,456
and carbon dioxide, as in yeast.

71
00:03:08,456 --> 00:03:09,996
This is fermentation.

72
00:03:10,446 --> 00:03:15,379
With oxygen, it enters the mitochondria
and is broken down fully to carbon

73
00:03:15,379 --> 00:03:16,796
dioxide and water.

74
00:03:16,796 --> 00:03:18,810
This is aerobic respiration.

75
00:03:19,410 --> 00:03:20,310
Your turn.

76
00:03:20,301 --> 00:03:22,658
Does glycolysis itself need oxygen?

77
00:03:22,658 --> 00:03:23,969
Pause and decide.

78
00:03:27,169 --> 00:03:28,568
No, it does not!

79
00:03:28,568 --> 00:03:32,504
That is why even organisms
living without oxygen can run it.

80
00:03:33,154 --> 00:03:34,699
Now, a worked example.

81
00:03:34,699 --> 00:03:37,514
A cell breaks down three
glucose by glycolysis.

82
00:03:37,514 --> 00:03:38,776
Count the products.

83
00:03:39,126 --> 00:03:41,224
Each glucose gives two pyruvic acid.

84
00:03:41,224 --> 00:03:43,505
So three glucose give six pyruvic acid.

85
00:03:43,955 --> 00:03:45,965
Each glucose gives a net two ATP.

86
00:03:45,965 --> 00:03:47,394
So three give six ATP.

87
00:03:47,844 --> 00:03:49,671
Each glucose gives two NADH.

88
00:03:49,671 --> 00:03:51,265
So three give six NADH.

89
00:03:51,265 --> 00:03:52,614
Six, six and six.

90
00:03:53,264 --> 00:03:54,729
Now, the exam trap.

91
00:03:55,079 --> 00:03:58,834
Many students think glycolysis
happens inside the mitochondria.

92
00:03:59,234 --> 00:04:01,967
But glycolysis happens in the cytoplasm.

93
00:04:01,967 --> 00:04:06,543
Only pyruvic acid moves into the
mitochondria, and only when oxygen is

94
00:04:06,543 --> 00:04:07,443
there.

95
00:04:07,832 --> 00:04:09,004
Where is this asked?

96
00:04:09,004 --> 00:04:12,559
As statement questions on the steps
of glycolysis, its enzymes, and the

97
00:04:12,559 --> 00:04:13,459
ATP count.

98
00:04:13,843 --> 00:04:15,073
Exam level now.

99
00:04:15,073 --> 00:04:18,003
Which statement about
glycolysis is correct?

100
00:04:18,003 --> 00:04:19,285
Pause and solve.

101
00:04:24,685 --> 00:04:25,933
The answer is A.

102
00:04:25,933 --> 00:04:29,328
Two ATP are used and four
are made for each glucose.

103
00:04:29,828 --> 00:04:30,875
B is wrong.

104
00:04:30,875 --> 00:04:35,348
Glycolysis happens in the cytoplasm,
not the mitochondrial matrix.

105
00:04:35,848 --> 00:04:38,880
C is wrong, because
glycolysis needs no oxygen.

106
00:04:38,880 --> 00:04:42,991
D is wrong, because fructose
bisphosphate splits into two three carbon

107
00:04:42,991 --> 00:04:44,745
sugars, not pyruvic acid.

108
00:04:45,345 --> 00:04:46,886
Here is your recap.

109
00:04:46,886 --> 00:04:48,470
Screenshot this card.

110
00:04:51,670 --> 00:04:53,856
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

111
00:04:54,256 --> 00:04:55,156
One.

112
00:04:54,752 --> 00:04:57,490
How do plants exchange
gases without lungs?

113
00:04:58,390 --> 00:04:59,290
Two.

114
00:04:58,803 --> 00:05:01,006
Where in the cell does glycolysis happen?

115
00:05:01,906 --> 00:05:02,806
Three.

116
00:05:02,438 --> 00:05:05,854
What is the net gain of ATP
from one glucose in glycolysis?

117
00:05:06,954 --> 00:05:11,336
Next time: what does yeast do with
pyruvic acid when there is no oxygen?

118
00:05:11,336 --> 00:05:14,255
We find out in fermentation
and the Krebs cycle.
